Fix GDMS channel dropdown parsing - wrong response field names (v0.122)

v0.121 got /sub/list succeeding end-to-end (HTTP 200, no error), but
the channel dropdown still showed nothing, even though the account
genuinely has reseller channels. The response-parsing code had never
actually been checked against the doc's own worked example response,
only written from a plausible-sounding guess - the same mistake that
caused the last two bugs, just in a different part of the code.

Pulling the doc's success.examples field for /sub/list for the first
time (previously only its parameter names had been checked) shows:

  {
    "data": {
      "result": [{ "subEnterpriseId": 212, "enterpriseName": "xyye", ... }],
      "total": 1, "pages": 1, "pageSize": 20, "pageNum": 1
    },
    "msg": "", "retCode": 0
  }

Three mismatches against what the code expected:

1. The channel array is at data.result, not data directly, and not
   data.list/data.rows/data.content (the fallback keys checked) - so
   it was never found, and every lookup silently returned zero
   channels despite a fully successful call.
2. The channel name field is "enterpriseName", not
   "subEnterpriseName" - any channel that WAS found would have shown
   its own numeric ID as its name.
3. The success/failure field is "retCode", not "code" - isSuccess()
   checked the wrong name, so it always fell through to its "field
   missing, assume success" default. Harmless so far by coincidence,
   but meant a real GDMS business-logic failure (HTTP 200, retCode
   non-zero) would never have been caught.

Also checked /assign's own example while in this code and found a
related issue: its response can report individual MACs as rejected
(data.result.errorMacList) even when the batch call itself succeeds
(retCode: 0) - previously ignored entirely, so a rejected MAC would
still get marked synced and never retried. assignMacsToChannel() now
excludes those MACs from what it reports as accepted.

gdms-api-findings.md (project doc) updated with the confirmed response
shape and a repeated lesson: check a doc entry's own example response,
not just its parameter names, before writing the parsing code.

